Perfect — here’s a 1-shot SSH-ready setup for any new Linux user so keys work reliably from the start:

SSH Setup Template for a New User
# Replace <username> with the actual user
# Replace <public_key_file> with your local public key path

# 1️⃣ Ensure user exists
sudo id <username> &>/dev/null || sudo adduser <username>

# 2️⃣ Ensure home directory exists
sudo mkdir -p /home/<username>
sudo chown <username>:<username> /home/<username>
sudo chmod 700 /home/<username>

# 3️⃣ Ensure .ssh directory exists
sudo mkdir -p /home/<username>/.ssh
sudo chown <username>:<username> /home/<username>/.ssh
sudo chmod 700 /home/<username>/.ssh

# 4️⃣ Add your public key to authorized_keys
sudo sh -c "cat <public_key_file> >> /home/<username>/.ssh/authorized_keys"

# 5️⃣ Fix ownership & permissions
sudo chown <username>:<username> /home/<username>/.ssh/authorized_keys
sudo chmod 600 /home/<username>/.ssh/authorized_keys

# 6️⃣ Convert line endings to Unix format (important if created on Windows)
sudo dos2unix /home/<username>/.ssh/authorized_keys

✅ Notes / Best Practices

.ssh → 700, authorized_keys → 600, owned by the user.

Convert line endings if keys are created on Windows (dos2unix).

Append keys (>>) instead of overwriting (>), to avoid losing existing keys.

Ensure the user’s home folder is not world-writable.

This is essentially a plug-and-play sequence for any Linux user, and it will make public-key authentication work on first attempt.

